    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: **Do Not Accept as Is \u2014 Negotiate**\n\nYou are in a position of maximum leverage: you were headhunted, you are happy at Meta, your current compensation is strong, and you have no urgent reason to leave. \n\nWhile Google\u2019s offer is solid, **it is an opening offer with clear room for improvement**. It currently includes a slight cut in base pay, leaves equity room on the table for an L6 PM in the Bay Area, and omits a sign-on bonus entirely.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown vs. Current Meta Comp\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google L6 (Initial Offer) | Notes / Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Below your current base; range is $183k\u2013$271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** (Google L6 standard target is 20%) |\n| **Annual Equity (Avg)** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 ($1.08M / 4 yrs) | **+$20,000/yr** (on a straight-line average) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** (Standard opening omission) |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$638,400\\*** | **+$112,400** (Frontloaded equity) |\n| **4-Year Avg Total Comp**| **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+$26,000/yr** |\n\n*\\*Google typically uses a frontloaded vesting schedule for new hires (commonly **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**). At 33% in Year 1, your first-year equity vest would be ~$356,400, boosting Year 1 cash flow. However, in Years 3 and 4, the initial grant drops significantly, meaning you rely heavily on refreshers to prevent a compensation cliff.*\n\n---\n\n### Where Google Left Money on the Table\n\n1. **Base Salary is Lower Than Current:** It makes little sense to take a pay cut on guaranteed base salary when leaving a secure role, especially since their posted range goes up to **$271,000**.\n2. **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses ($50,000\u2013$100,000+) to offset unvested equity left behind at competitors like Meta.\n3. **Equity Band Room:** For an L6 PM in San Francisco/Bay Area, competitive top-of-band initial equity grants can reach **$1.2M to $1.35M+ over 4 years**.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Proposal)\n\nAsk for adjustments across all three core levers:\n\n* **Base Salary:** **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000**\n  * *Reasoning:* Matches/exceeds your Meta base while staying comfortably within their $183k\u2013$271k band.\n* **Sign-On Bonus:** **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n  * *Reasoning:* Bridges the transition risk and compensates for walking away from your upcoming Meta vesting cycles and unvested equity.\n* **Equity (GSUs):** **$1,240,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 over 4 years** (e.g., ~$310k\u2013$325k/yr average)\n  * *Reasoning:* Pushes your total annualized package past the $600k mark and provides greater downside protection against stock fluctuations.\n\n---\n\n### How to Deliver the Counter (Script / Strategy)\n\nBecause you are happy where you are, your posture should be **warm, appreciative, and clear about what makes the move a decisive \"yes.\"**\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I enjoyed meeting the team and am genuinely excited about the scope of this L6 role. Because I wasn\u2019t actively looking and am in a great spot at Meta with upcoming vesting cycles and strong performance, leaving requires the financial package to be clearly compelling.*\n> \n> *Right now, the base salary represents a step down from my current $240,000 base, and there is no sign-on bonus to bridge the transition. To make this an easy decision for me to sign, I\u2019d be looking for:*\n> \n> 1. *A base salary of **$255,000***\n> 2. *An equity grant of **$1,280,000** over 4 years*\n> 3. *A first-year sign-on bonus of **$75,000***\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Strategic Considerations Beyond Compensation\n\n* **The Equity Cliff vs. Refresher Culture:** Google's front-loaded vesting means your Year 1 and Year 2 compensation will look inflated. Even though refreshers stack, they depend on performance ratings and macroeconomic grant budgets. Treat unvested refreshers as variable, not guaranteed.\n* **Meta PM vs. Google PM Dynamics:** \n  * Meta tends to be highly top-down, execution-heavy, and PM-driven.\n  * Google is historically more engineering-led and consensus-driven, requiring more stakeholder management and alignment to ship products. Make sure you are comfortable with this cultural shift.",
