    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line: **Financially, this is a strong offer \u2014 but whether you take it depends on your promotion trajectory and cultural preference.**\n\nGoogle moved significantly: they bumped equity by **+$200,000** (hitting the upper band for L6) and matched your Meta base salary at **$240,000**. While they held firm on the sign-on bonus, **the frontloaded equity grant more than offsets the unvested Meta RSUs you would walk away from.**\n\n---\n\n### The Updated Numbers (Year-by-Year Reality)\n\nGoogle almost universally vests new-hire equity on a frontloaded schedule (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**):\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **Equity Vest ($1.28M total)** | $422,400 *(33%)* | $422,400 *(33%)* | $281,600 *(22%)* | $153,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Annual Total Comp** | **~$710,400** | **~$710,400** | **~$569,600\\*** | **~$441,600\\*** |\n\n*\\*Note on Years 3 & 4:* Google stacks annual refreshers on top of this. With standard performance, refreshers typically add $60k\u2013$100k+/year by Years 3 and 4, buffering against the cliff.\n\n#### Comparison to Staying at Meta:\n* **Meta (Status Quo):** ~$526,000/year (with $200,000 vesting over the next 12 months).\n* **Google Year 1:** **~$710,400** \u2014 a **+$184,000 net gain in your first year alone**, which directly makes up for the unvested $200,000 you leave behind at Meta.\n* **4-Year Average at Google:** **~$608,000/year** (a ~$82,000/year increase over your current baseline, excluding refreshers and stock appreciation).\n\n---\n\n### Key Factors to Decide\n\n#### 1. Why You Should TAKE IT\n* **Immediate Cash Flow Boost:** You will take home roughly **~$360,000+ more over the first two years** at Google compared to Meta.\n* **Resume & Career Capital:** Having Senior PM (L6 / IC6) track records at both Meta and Google provides broad industry mobility.\n* **Work-Life Balance (WLB):** While team-dependent, Google generally offers a more sustainable pace and lower churn/re-org friction than Meta.\n\n#### 2. Why You Should DECLINE & Stay at Meta\n* **You are happy and have political capital:** Rebuilding trust, domain context, and stakeholder networks at Google takes 6\u201312 months of high effort.\n* **Meta PM vs. Google PM Culture:** \n  * Meta PMs have strong ownership, high execution velocity, and top-down mandate.\n  * Google PMs operate in an engineering-led, consensus-driven culture where shipping features often requires navigating complex stakeholder reviews and matrixed approvals.\n* **Proximity to IC7/Director:** If you are within 12\u201318 months of an IC7 promotion at Meta, an IC7 compensation package ($750k\u2013$900k+) easily beats starting over as a tenured L6 at Google.\n\n---\n\n### What to Do Next\n\n1. **Acknowledge the Final Offer:** Google rarely moves past this point without a formal competing counteroffer in writing. Pushing again for a sign-on bonus when they already gave $200k in equity and base protection will likely result in a polite \"this is our final stance.\"\n2. **Team & Scope Due Diligence:** If you haven\u2019t already, ask for 30 minutes with the hiring manager or a peer PM on the team to evaluate:\n   * What is the team's charter, visibility, and track record of launching products?\n   * How engineering-heavy is decision-making in this specific org?\n3. **Make the Call:**\n   * **Accept** if maximizing 2-year total compensation (~$1.4M over 24 months) and diversifying your FAANG pedigree is worth the friction of ramping up at a new company.\n   * **Decline** if you value your established leverage at Meta and are on track for IC7.",
